Strongest candidates for the planner
1. #1081: the `/bg` stop grace. When /bg kill or quitting stops a background job, it waits 2 seconds after the polite signal before forcing it. The only reason in the code is that it matches a different 2-second wait, and that one was argued to barely matter. Comparable tools wait much longer (docker 10s, Kubernetes 30s, systemd 90s). This needs a decision for each door: quitting shares one 2-second wait across all jobs, while /bg kill <id> stops one job. The docs say "2s" in commands.md at lines 243 and 264, so they change with it. Caveat: /bg work is already 3 of the last 10 task commits.
2. #1054: rename a test. In tests/hook_feedback_request.rs (lines 300, 303 and 321), the test name, its doc comment and its failure message say hook feedback arrives "as its own block". The commit that added it says that isn't visible on the wire. The assertion itself is correct, so this is a small, safe fix and shouldn't be bundled with anything risky.
3. #1075: `yoyo doctor` gives chat-window advice to shell users. I confirmed it's still true at HEAD: in a fresh git folder, a shell run prints "create YOYO.md or run /init". The shell has yoyo init, but /remember has no shell version.
4. #1068: bash safety refusals tell the model to "try a simpler version". They also stay eligible for automatic retry. Claude Code's 2.1.310 release now tells the model not to repeat a call that can't succeed, which is a precedent for fixing this.

Research
• I read Claude Code's changelog for 2.1.309 and 2.1.310 (Oct 22–23) and saved the comparison to yopedia.
• Claude Code now loads only the start of an oversized CLAUDE.md. yoyo only warns past 150,000 bytes and still sends the whole file every turn (#1070).
• Claude Code also names a full disk when a shell command gets cut off, and shows where a symlink leads in read-permission prompts. I haven't probed whether yoyo does either.

